Let \(F: \mathcal{C} \rightarrow \mathcal{D}\) be a functor. Recall that \(\mathcal{D}\text{-Set}\) denotes the category of functors \(\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow \mathbf{Set}\) (i.e. presheaves on \(\mathcal{D}\)), with natural transformations as morphisms. Likewise \(\mathcal{C}\text{-Set} = \mathbf{Set}^{\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{op}}}\).

## Definition of the pullback functor

The **pullback functor** (also called the **inverse image functor** along \(F\)) is

\[
\Delta_{F} : \mathcal{D}\text{-Set} \longrightarrow \mathcal{C}\text{-Set},
\]

defined by **precomposition** with \(F\). Concretely:

### Action on objects

For a presheaf \(P \in \mathcal{D}\text{-Set}\), i.e. a functor \(P : \mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow \mathbf{Set}\), we set

\[
\Delta_{F}(P) := P \circ F^{\mathrm{op}} : \mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow \mathbf{Set},
\]

where \(F^{\mathrm{op}} : \mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow \mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{op}}\) is the functor induced by \(F\) on opposite categories (it acts on objects and morphisms identically to \(F\), only the direction of the source/target data is dualized).

Unpacking this on objects of \(\mathcal{C}\): for every object \(c \in \mathrm{Ob}(\mathcal{C})\),

\[
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(c) \;=\; P\bigl(F(c)\bigr).
\]

Unpacking on morphisms of \(\mathcal{C}\): for every arrow \(\alpha : c' \rightarrow c\) in \(\mathcal{C}\) (so \(\alpha^{\mathrm{op}} : c \rightarrow c'\) in \(\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{op}}\)),

\[
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(\alpha^{\mathrm{op}})
\;=\;
P\bigl(F(\alpha)^{\mathrm{op}}\bigr)
\;=\;
P\bigl(F(\alpha)\bigr) : P(F(c)) \longrightarrow P(F(c')).
\]

Equivalently, writing the restriction map of \(\Delta_{F}(P)\) along \(\alpha : c' \rightarrow c\) as

\[
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(\alpha) : \bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(c) \longrightarrow \bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(c'),
\]

we have \(\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(\alpha) = P\bigl(F(\alpha)\bigr)\), the restriction map of \(P\) along the arrow \(F(\alpha) : F(c') \rightarrow F(c)\) in \(\mathcal{D}\).

The presheaf axioms for \(\Delta_{F}(P)\) follow immediately from those of \(P\) together with the functoriality of \(F\):
- \(\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(\mathrm{id}_{c}) = P(F(\mathrm{id}_{c})) = P(\mathrm{id}_{F(c)}) = \mathrm{id}_{P(F(c))}\),
- \(\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(\beta\circ\alpha) = P(F(\beta\circ\alpha)) = P(F(\beta)\circ F(\alpha)) = P(F(\alpha))\circ P(F(\beta))\),

so \(\Delta_{F}(P)\) is indeed a well-defined object of \(\mathcal{C}\text{-Set}\).

### Action on morphisms

Let \(\eta : P \Rightarrow Q\) be a morphism in \(\mathcal{D}\text{-Set}\), i.e. a natural transformation between presheaves \(P, Q : \mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow \mathbf{Set}\). We define

\[
\Delta_{F}(\eta) : \Delta_{F}(P) \Rightarrow \Delta_{F}(Q)
\]

by setting, for each object \(c \in \mathcal{C}\),

\[
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(\eta)\bigr)_{c} \;:=\; \eta_{F(c)} : P(F(c)) \longrightarrow Q(F(c)).
\]

**Naturality check.** For every arrow \(\alpha : c' \rightarrow c\) in \(\mathcal{C}\) we must verify commutativity of

\[
\begin{array}{ccc}
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(c) & \xrightarrow{\;(\Delta_{F}\eta)_{c}\;} & \bigl(\Delta_{F}(Q)\bigr)(c) \\[4pt]
\Big\downarrow{\scriptstyle (\Delta_{F}P)(\alpha)} & & \Big\downarrow{\scriptstyle (\Delta_{F}Q)(\alpha)} \\[4pt]
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(P)\bigr)(c') & \xrightarrow{\;(\Delta_{F}\eta)_{c'}\;} & \bigl(\Delta_{F}(Q)\bigr)(c')
\end{array}
\]

i.e. \((\Delta_{F}Q)(\alpha)\circ\eta_{F(c)} = \eta_{F(c')}\circ(\Delta_{F}P)(\alpha)\). Using the definitions above, the left-hand side is \(Q(F(\alpha))\circ\eta_{F(c)}\) and the right-hand side is \(\eta_{F(c')}\circ P(F(\alpha))\). These are equal precisely because \(\eta\) is natural at the arrow \(F(\alpha) : F(c') \rightarrow F(c)\) in \(\mathcal{D}\). Hence \(\Delta_{F}(\eta)\) is a natural transformation \(\Delta_{F}(P) \Rightarrow \Delta_{F}(Q)\).

### Functoriality

For composable morphisms \(\eta : P \Rightarrow Q\), \(\theta : Q \Rightarrow R\) in \(\mathcal{D}\text{-Set}\) and for each \(c \in \mathcal{C}\),

\[
\bigl(\Delta_{F}(\theta\circ\eta)\bigr)_{c} = (\theta\circ\eta)_{F(c)} = \theta_{F(c)}\circ\eta_{F(c)} = (\Delta_{F}\theta)_{c}\circ(\Delta_{F}\eta)_{c},
\]

so \(\Delta_{F}(\theta\circ\eta) = \Delta_{F}(\theta)\circ\Delta_{F}(\eta)\). Also \(\bigl(\Delta_{F}(\mathrm{id}_{P})\bigr)_{c} = (\mathrm{id}_{P})_{F(c)} = \mathrm{id}_{P(F(c))} = \mathrm{id}_{(\Delta_{F}P)(c)}\), hence \(\Delta_{F}(\mathrm{id}_{P}) = \mathrm{id}_{\Delta_{F}(P)}\).

Therefore \(\Delta_{F}\) preserves identities and composition, and is a genuine functor \(\mathcal{D}\text{-Set} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}\text{-Set}\).

## Summary

The pullback functor \(\Delta_{F} : \mathcal{D}\text{-Set} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}\text{-Set}\) is the functor given by precomposition with \(F^{\mathrm{op}}\):

- **On objects** \(P \in \mathcal{D}\text{-Set}\): \(\Delta_{F}(P) = P \circ F^{\mathrm{op}}\), i.e. the presheaf on \(\mathcal{C}\) with \((\Delta_{F}P)(c) = P(F(c))\) and restriction along \(\alpha : c' \rightarrow c\) given by \((\Delta_{F}P)(\alpha) = P(F(\alpha))\).
- **On morphisms** \(\eta : P \Rightarrow Q\): \(\Delta_{F}(\eta)\) is the natural transformation with components \((\Delta_{F}\eta)_{c} = \eta_{F(c)}\) for every \(c \in \mathcal{C}\).

This construction is sometimes denoted \(F^{*}\) or \(\mathrm{res}_{F}\) ("restriction of presheaves along \(F\)"), and it is the functor part of the essential pullback of presheaf categories along \(F\); it admits left and right adjoints (the left and right Kan extensions along \(F\)).

